{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>For evil is before your faces</b> — as its Targum renders. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 10:10). But it would have been better had he explained it to us, and the textual variants of the Targum are confused on this point. In some of them it is written: \"for the evil that you intend to do against your faces is evident.\" Now, according to this version, it appears that Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Exodus 10:10) intended to explain: the evil that you plan to do — there is <b>before your faces</b> testimony to bear witness against you, that you intend to flee, in the language of \"and they shall set two men, sons of Belial, before him, and they shall testify against him, saying\" (I Kings 21:10), and the Aramaic translation of וְהוֹשִׁיבוּ (\"they shall set\") is אִסְתַּחֲרוּ (\"they stationed\"). Likewise \"and they sat to eat bread\" (Genesis 37:28) is rendered אִסְתַּחַר, and \"arise, sit\" (Genesis 27:19) is rendered אִסְתַּחַר. And in other versions it is written \"against your faces, to be evident,\" meaning: this evil is destined to turn back against your faces, for upon you it will turn, from the language of \"and no inheritance shall turn\" (Numbers 36:7), rendered לֹא תִסְתַּחַר (\"it shall not turn\").\n<br>This is like the midrash of the Sages, who said in Shemot Rabbah (13:5): the way of young men and elders to sacrifice — perhaps the little ones and the children? Whoever says this thing has in mind only to flee. See, for that evil which you seek to do, that you intend in your hearts to flee — it will turn back <b>before your faces</b>, so that you will not leave here, that is to say, measure for measure. And I have also found in it yet another version: \"there is not against your faces to be evident\" — he says: there is nothing in the appearance of your faces to indicate the evil that is in your hearts, for the appearance of your faces testifies against you. And according to the plain sense: know that <b>for evil is before your faces</b> — it stands and is close to coming upon you from me, for I will deal out evil to you when I see that you desire to flee."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Aramaic words from Onkelos are left transliterated where Ramban quotes them to argue from the Aramaic root correspondence. The phrase מִי שֶׁאוֹמֵר דָּבָר זֶה is rendered as a continuation of Pharaoh's speech per the midrash cited."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:05:36.932564727Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}